<h1>Evaluate AI Like a QA Engineer</h1>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/79e89380152c3b12fbf531ce36771480-image-1786377947455.png" alt="A QA engineer reviewing code and test results on a computer screen" style="width: 35%; max-width: 200px; border-radius: 10px;">
  <p style="flex: 1;">A QA Engineer, quality assurance engineer, tests software to find bugs before users do. They check that features work as expected, document what's broken, and decide what's urgent. That's the exact skill you need whenever a student hands you AI-generated code, not writing it yourself, but judging whether what AI wrote actually does the job.</p>
</div>

<p>A QA Engineer's main deliverable is the bug report: what was expected, what actually happened, and why it matters. Below, you'll play that role on three real prompt-and-code pairs, asking three different questions a QA Engineer would ask: does the output match what was asked, does the code actually work, and can you explain why it works.</p>

---

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
<iframe src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/Unit5AIF2/evaluate-ai-qa.html" width="100%" height="720" style="border:none;" title="Evaluate AI Like a QA Engineer: three real bug reports"></iframe>
</div>


